 "answer": "Air quality readings in Kuching are generally in the **Moderate** range (with Air Pollutant Index / API readings hovering around the 70–100 mark). \n\nWhether it is safe for you to cycle depends on your personal health profile:\n\n* **If you are generally healthy:** Moderate air quality means the air is acceptable for most people. A casual evening ride should be fine, though you might notice reduced visibility or a slight smell of haze. \n* **If you belong to sensitive groups:** If you have asthma, pre-existing heart or lung conditions, allergies, or if you are elderly or children, **it is best to avoid or significantly cut down on heavy outdoor exercise** like cycling. Strenuous physical activity causes you to breathe faster and deeper, pulling more fine particulate matter (PM2.5) into your lungs.\n\n**Tips if you still decide to go out:**\n* Keep your ride light, short, and at a moderate pace rather than pushing for a heavy workout.\n* Check the real-time API updates via official channels (like Malaysia’s APIMS or the Natural Resources and Environment Board Sarawak) right before heading out, as conditions can shift.\n* If your eyes or throat start feeling irritated, cut the ride short and head indoors.",
